<main id="story">
 <section class="intro container"><div class="section-index">01 / THE QUESTION</div><div class="intro-grid"><div><p class="kicker">THE SAME SCORE. A VERY DIFFERENT GAME.</p><h2>Is a seven-point deficit always a seven-point deficit?</h2></div><div class="intro-right"><p>With twelve minutes left, a trailing team has room to run a normal offense. With sixty seconds left, it must create opportunities almost immediately. The scoreboard shows the same gap, but the game state is fundamentally different.</p><p>We follow the interaction between <strong>score deficit</strong> and <strong>time remaining</strong> to understand how tactics change—and whether urgency aligns with historical comeback outcomes.</p></div></div>
 <div class="versus-grid"><div class="scenario-card"><span class="scenario-tag">SCENARIO A</span><div class="scenario-clock">12:00</div><span class="scenario-annotation">Fourth quarter begins</span><div class="scenario-rule"></div><p>More possessions remain. Teams can still prioritize high-quality looks and patient offense.</p></div><div class="versus-center">VS</div><div class="scenario-card late"><span class="scenario-tag">SCENARIO B</span><div class="scenario-clock">01:00</div><span class="scenario-annotation">Final minute</span><div class="scenario-rule"></div><p>Fewer chances remain. Quick attempts and risk-taking may become necessary, even as the path to a win narrows.</p></div></div>
 </section>
